From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH v6 0/2] microblaze: fix and test the entry.S argument save area

MicroBlaze arch assembly calls C at seventeen sites with r1 pointing at
live saved state, so the ABI's caller-reserved argument save area --
[caller_sp + 4, caller_sp + 28), which a callee may spill its incoming
argument registers into -- aliases data the kernel needs back.  At the
syscall dispatch it aliases pt_regs + 4 == PT_R1, the saved user stack
pointer, so a syscall handler that spills its first argument silently
overwrites the SP userspace resumes with.  A GCC 15 register-allocation
change made such spills common, turning the latent bug fatal: init dies
with AT_FDCWD as its stack pointer.

Patch 1 reserves the argument save area (C_ARG_SIZE) around every C call
on a kernel-entry path.  Patch 2 adds a selftest that asserts the property
the fix restores -- a syscall does not change the user stack pointer -- so
the class of bug cannot silently return.

The two are independent: patch 1 is the fix and stands alone; patch 2 is a
regression test that can be reviewed and applied on its own.

Validation:
  - patch 1 boots a Buildroot glibc userland (GCC 15.3, Linux 6.12.81) to
    a shell on the exact configuration that hangs without it, on QEMU and
    on hardware.
  - the reverse also holds: reverting patch 1 and rebuilding with the same
    compiler reproduces the original hang at "Run /init".
  - patch 2 reports "ok 1 user stack pointer preserved across 100000
    syscalls" on the fixed kernel; on the reverted kernel the system does
    not reach userspace, so the test cannot pass -- it fails in the
    strongest possible way.

Changes since v5:
  - added the selftest (patch 2); renumbered the fix as patch 1.
  - hardware validation of the fix (v5 was QEMU-only for the boot path).

Sam Price (2):
  microblaze: reserve the ABI argument save area when calling C from
    entry.S
  selftests/microblaze: add kernel-entry ABI regression test

